course: dictionary
lesson: 1155
title: Brilliantly: three meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Brilliantly» is an adverb. An adverb tells you how something is done, so it often comes after a verb: «She played brilliantly.» It describes the playing. This word comes from the adjective «brilliant», and adverbs often end in -ly.
explain: One word can have more than one meaning, so read the words around it. «She played brilliantly» means very well. «The stars shone brilliantly» means very brightly. Check by swapping in a simpler word: «She played very well» still makes sense, so the first meaning fits.
explain: The third meaning is informal and used in the UK. «It worked brilliantly» means it worked really well, and the word makes the happy feeling stronger. You would not use it in a formal report, but you may hear it in everyday talk.
fact: «Brilliantly» means very well or very cleverly, as in «She played brilliantly».
fact: «Brilliantly» can also mean very brightly, as in «The stars shone brilliantly».
fact: In informal UK speech, «brilliantly» makes a word stronger in a happy way, as in «It worked brilliantly».
fact: «Brilliant» is the adjective, and «brilliantly» is the adverb made from it.
fact: Teachers often write «brilliantly» in comments on good work.
exercise: «She played brilliantly in the match.» What does brilliantly mean here? | Very quietly | Very slowly | *Very well | Very sadly
exercise: «The stars shone brilliantly over the dark hills.» What does brilliantly mean here? | Very politely | Very rudely | Very late | *Very brightly
exercise: Find and copy the adverb in «The bird sang brilliantly at dawn.» | dawn | *brilliantly | bird | sang
test: «It worked brilliantly, and everyone cheered.» In this sentence, brilliantly means: | Badly and slowly | Quietly | Very brightly, like a torch | *Very well, in an informal, happy way
test: Which pair is correct? | both words are nouns | *brilliant is the adjective, brilliantly is the adverb | brilliantly is the adjective, brilliant is the adverb | both words are adverbs
exam: Ruby writes «She swam brilliantly» in her story. What does this tell the reader? | She swam in the dark | *She swam very well | She swam very badly | She stopped swimming
